GAPBS: perform the serialized-graph load copy in user space.

Why
---
GAPBS reads its CSR with file.read() (reader.h ReadSerializedGraph), which is a
read(2) syscall: the kernel copies the bytes into the destination buffers. No
user-mode load or store executes for that copy, so Intel Pin cannot observe it.

M5's PAC and WAC sit at the memory controller and count it -- and a bulk load writes
every 64 B word of every destination page, which is the densest access pattern that
exists. For a 1.05 GB graph that is roughly 269,000 4 KB pages that M5 records as
64/64 words touched and a Pin-based tool records as zero.

Measured directly (src/profiler/validate/kernel_blindspot.c), moving 512 MiB:

    via read(2):  5 DRAM accesses,        4 pages,  1.250 words/page
    via memcpy:   24,838,154 accesses,  262,150 pages, 63.999 words/page

This patch routes the copy through a small user-space staging buffer, so the writes
to the destination arrays become ordinary stores that Pin sees. The bytes that end up
in memory, and their layout, are identical; only which agent performs the final copy
changes. The staging buffer is 4 MB, so it stays cache-resident and adds negligible
traffic of its own.

Use this to measure what M5's hardware would have seen. Leave it off to measure only
the application's own access pattern. Both are legitimate; they answer different
questions, and docs/methodology.md reports both.

Apply with:  git -C bench/gapbs apply ../../benchmarks/patches/gapbs-userspace-load.patch

--- a/src/reader.h
+++ b/src/reader.h
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 #include "pvector.h"
 #include "util.h"

+#include <vector>

 /*
 GAP Benchmark Suite
@@ -40,6 +41,29 @@ class Reader {
   std::string filename_;

  public:
+  // Copy `bytes` from `in` into `dst` through a user-space staging buffer.
+  //
+  // file.read(dst, bytes) would have the kernel write dst directly, which Intel Pin
+  // cannot observe (see benchmarks/patches/gapbs-userspace-load.patch). Staging the
+  // data and memcpy-ing it makes the destination writes ordinary user-mode stores,
+  // so a memory-reference profiler sees the same traffic a memory controller would.
+  //
+  // Identical bytes, identical layout; only the agent performing the final copy
+  // differs. The 4 MB staging buffer stays cache-resident.
+  static void ReadUserspace(std::ifstream &in, char *dst, std::streamsize bytes) {
+    const std::streamsize kChunk = 4 << 20;
+    std::vector<char> staging(static_cast<size_t>(kChunk));
+    std::streamsize done = 0;
+    while (done < bytes) {
+      std::streamsize want = bytes - done;
+      if (want > kChunk) want = kChunk;
+      in.read(staging.data(), want);
+      std::streamsize got = in.gcount();
+      if (got <= 0) break;
+      memcpy(dst + done, staging.data(), static_cast<size_t>(got));
+      done += got;
+    }
+  }
+
   explicit Reader(std::string filename) : filename_(filename) {}

   std::string GetSuffix() {
@@ -287,13 +311,13 @@ class Reader {
     DestID_ *neighs = new DestID_[num_edges];
     DestID_ **index = CSRGraph<NodeID_, DestID_>::GenIndex(offsets, neighs);
-    file.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(offsets.data()), num_index_bytes);
-    file.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(neighs), num_neigh_bytes);
+    ReadUserspace(file, reinterpret_cast<char*>(offsets.data()), num_index_bytes);
+    ReadUserspace(file, reinterpret_cast<char*>(neighs), num_neigh_bytes);
     if (directed && invert) {
       pvector<SGOffset> inv_offsets(num_nodes+1);
       DestID_ *inv_neighs = new DestID_[num_edges];
       DestID_ **inv_index = CSRGraph<NodeID_, DestID_>::GenIndex(inv_offsets,
                                                                 inv_neighs);
-      file.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(inv_offsets.data()), num_index_bytes);
-      file.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(inv_neighs), num_neigh_bytes);
+      ReadUserspace(file, reinterpret_cast<char*>(inv_offsets.data()), num_index_bytes);
+      ReadUserspace(file, reinterpret_cast<char*>(inv_neighs), num_neigh_bytes);
       file.close();
       return CSRGraph<NodeID_, DestID_, invert>(num_nodes, index, neighs,
                                                 inv_index, inv_neighs);
